                          The Immortal Struggle v1.25
        Created, programmed and copyright (C)1994 by Chris W. Rea [UL]
     A multiplayer online game for BBX, DLG-Pro, FalconCBCS, and Xenolink
                            Release Date: 09-Apr-94


              "TIS_Changes.doc" -- Release History (Newest First)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

09-Apr-94 / v1.25
-----------------
     Third  and probably final release.  T.I.S. is  hopefully  completely  both
Enforcer-clean  and  bug-free.  If you come across any problems, please send me
email.  The  following  major  and  minor  changes have occurred since the last
public release, v1.07:

- added the number of times a player has played to the LPL utility.

- fixed a minor bug with SQ in that it would duplicated the last question in a
  file if the file ended with a separator bar

- added support for keyfiles (which all registered users are entitled to.)

- added a cap on the # of bytes the program will seek for in the questions file
  if a keyfile is not found; it's in the range of 30K or so. Register!

- added the CONFIGURE command, which allows the Sysop (or an assigned game
  administrator) to configure important game parameters and the Level and Sense
  of most incantations to their likings. This can make for interesting games.

- modified the new player routine so that instead of tacking the player onto
  the end of the player file, it would seek for either an empty record or for
  a player who has not played for more than 14 days, and then overwrite that
  record. This will stop the game from getting too cluttered with inactive
  players. (So it violates the "immortal" part; sue me. I had one or two
  complaints. :-)

- added four new POWERFUL incantations to make the game much more competitive
  and interesting, and to give lower level entities a bit more ability to do
  something really heinous to higher level entities. I won't say anything more
  than the new incantation names: LIGHT, PERSUADE, STEAL, and TORNADO. Enjoy.
  Thanks to Mark Thurgood for helping me decide what would be good to make the
  game more competitive.

     REMEMBER!  Registering  gets  you  a  LARGE  QUESTIONS FILE! We're talking
160K  or   more  worth  of  questions!  More  than  2500 of them! And remember,
to use anything more than 30K worth of a questions file, you need a keyfile...

12-Feb-93 / v1.07
-----------------
     Second  release.   This  can  be  considered a "major" release because now
there is a BBX-specific version of The Immortal Struggle included as well.  The
BBX-specific  executable  is  called  "TISBBX"  to  differentiate  between  the
DLG-Pro/FalconCBCS/Xenolink  version,  which  is  now called "TISDFX".  The BBX
version  of  T.I.S.   has  a NODE utilities command, which also shows up in the
other  version  but disabled.  The ANSI command in the BBX version is disabled,
since  BBX  offers superior ANSI colour mapping control.  The BBX version takes
advantage of which ever text editor the user has configured his account to use,
instead of using the original less capable T.I.S.  message editor.

     Also  new  and included in this release are two programs, "SQ", a question
file  sorter  and  validation  utility,  and  "LPL", a program to list players'
levels  to  determine a contest winner.  Please see the documentation for these
programs for more information.

     Lastly,  there have been a few minor modifications to T.I.S.  itself.  The
player  data  and  mountain data are no longer written as often; superior break
condition  handling  has  reduced  the need for writing these files as often as
they  were written before.  The executable has also become slightly smaller and
more  optimal  as  a result of the work to convert T.I.S.  to take advantage of
BBX.   A  small  display  bug or two fixed and a couple of other trivial things
also changed.

02-Jan-93 / v1.00
-----------------
     Initial  release  of  The  Immortal Struggle.  DLG-Pro/FalconCBCS/Xenolink
version  only  was  released.  No bugs were reported, which is good, and I also
got a few registrations, which is also good.  But I'd like to see more.  :-)


